// General `exists` (NOT the `let`-replacement mode): the existential
// correlates two DIFFERENT predicates through its bound variable — "some
// single user both logged in and made a transfer in the last hour". The
// join on `u` is the whole point; a broken join that accepted "some login
// AND some (other-user) transfer" would wrongly permit the second Alert.

permit (
    principal,
    action == Drupe::Action::"Alert",
    resource
)
when temporal {
    exists (u: String). (formerly within 1h Drupe::Action::"Login"::request{ input.user: u } && formerly within 1h Drupe::Action::"Transfer"::request{ input.user: u })};
